  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Washington has $175M in federal contract obligations in FY2027 to date, spread across 1,146 awards with an average award size of $153K. Obligations fell 99.1% year over year. Washington obligations peaked in earlier years, including $19.4B in FY26, $15.8B in FY25, and $15.4B in FY24, and then dropped to $175M in FY27 to date. The Department of Energy is the largest buyer in FY2027 to date with $108M in obligations, followed by the Department of Veterans Affairs with $41.6M. HANFORD TANK WASTE OPERATIONS & CLOSURE, LLC is the top vendor with $100M, and Hazardous Waste Treatment and Disposal is the largest industry by dollar value at $100M. The FY2027 to date picture shows concentrated spending tied to a few large awards and buyers, and much lower total obligations than the recent fiscal-year totals above.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "What explains the FY2027 to date drop in obligations?",
        "body": "The fiscal-year comparison shows obligations fell 99.1% year over year. The Department of Energy accounted for $108M of the FY2027 to date total of $175M. HANFORD TANK WASTE OPERATIONS & CLOSURE, LLC received $100M, and Hazardous Waste Treatment and Disposal accounted for $100M in industry obligations. The concentration of dollars in a few large awards and the much larger FY26 total of $19.4B help explain the sharp year-over-year decline."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies, vendors, and industries dominate FY2027 to date spending?",
        "body": "The Department of Energy leads with $108M in obligations, followed by the Department of Veterans Affairs with $41.6M and the Department of Health and Human Services with $8.2M. HANFORD TANK WASTE OPERATIONS & CLOSURE, LLC is the top vendor with $100M, and T-MOBILE USA, INC. is second with $14.7M. Hazardous Waste Treatment and Disposal is the top industry with $100M, followed by Wireless Telecommunications Carriers (except Satellite) with $14.6M and Plumbing, Heating, and Air-Conditioning Contractors with $11.1M."
      }
    ]
  },
